  "kind": "rerecord",
  "why": "The Mac's SSH setup after login takes fewer round trips. Each helper the app starts (the greeting probe, then connect) now runs one /bin/sh command that probes the platform (uname), checks the cached helper and execs it (SSHBootstrapScript.launch), replacing the separate platform probe and cache check exec channels; a cache without the helper says so on stderr (DISPATCH_PLATFORM=..., status 3) and the app uploads, then launches again. The waiting login is released through the verified probe (new feature operation login.release, the same checks as the publish script in Rust) instead of a publish exec channel, and the first connect starts as the release is sent. Recordings lose two or three open.command requests (the uname probe, the cache check, the publish script), their helper launches carry other text, the probe's hello names login.release and its connection gains a request, and the app observes every bundled helper's payload: removed and added requests cannot be rewritten, so every test whose recording holds the old setup is recorded again. The list is every corpus test directory whose captures hold the recorded uname probe command (DISPATCH_PLATFORM=%s:%s), the old probe launch (--remote --capabilities) or a connect launch (connect --session): 168 tests from a scan of all 3358 corpus captures (zstd -dc, byte search); the three SSHBootstrapPolicyTests that run the unchanged publish script directly keep their recordings. Helper captures replay unchanged: no recorded client named the new feature, and a helper that is not a --remote --stdio probe never accepts it.",
